# Blog Post Final Check — URL Sanitization

When the user asks for a **final check**, **ready to publish**, or **pre-publish review** on any blog post, always remind them to replace real or example URLs embedded in code blocks (e.g. SQL query results, curl examples, dataset paths) with safe fake placeholders.

## Why

Real URLs in code output may point to content that cannot be vetted — including adult content, defunct domains, or third-party sites whose content may change after publish.

## What to check

- SQL query result rows containing URLs (e.g. `┌─URL─...`)
- `curl` command examples with third-party hostnames
- Dataset paths pointing to real S3 buckets or external services that are not owned by the author

## How to fix

Replace real URLs with clearly fictional placeholders:

```
❌ http://bdsm_po_yers=0&with_video
✅ http://example-query-result.com

❌ https://datasets-documentation.s3.amazonaws.com/...  (if not author-controlled)
✅ Leave S3 paths as-is only if the bucket is confirmed author-controlled
```

Adjust row counts to plausible round numbers if the original value came from the replaced URL. Also, use .com or .io domains.
